On the instruction tables
-------------------------

`tables/*.tab2` map each processor's mnemonics and addressing modes to the bytes
it encodes. Those encodings are facts about the target processors, published in
the manufacturers' own data sheets: `ADD A,R0 = 0x28` on an 8051 is not anyone's
creative work, and stating it is not copying anyone's expression of it.

The tables were mechanically converted from the format used by TASM, whose own
copyright notice lists its `.TAB` files among those that "may be freely copied
and shared with others" — separately from its C source files, which it
restricts. The conversion carries over only the structural data — mnemonic,
operand pattern, opcode, byte counts, encoding rule and class — and discards
every line of commentary, so no upstream revision header, authorship credit or
prose survives in them. `tab1to2` performs the conversion and its source is in
this repository, so anyone can repeat it and see what it does and does not keep.

The result is that the BSD licence above applies cleanly to the whole of this
repository. There is no third-party material here held under other terms.

On compatibility with TASM
--------------------------

tabasm is an independent implementation of the Telemark Assembler (TASM),
written from a behavioural specification rather than from TASM's source, which
its authors have not read. It aims to be output-compatible with TASM, and names
it for that reason. The Telemark Assembler is the work of its own authors; this
project claims no association with them or with their trade names.
